WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.610
Film Courage: You don’t need luck to be
a successful screenwriter?

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:07.450
Aren’t there people who luck into it or
it just seems that way?

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:12.170
CSUN Professor, Eric Edson: I’m one of those
people who believes in you create your own

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:13.980
luck.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.340
Is luck a factor?

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:22.480
In anything, the stars sometimes have to come
into alignment before something becomes possible.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:29.460
But the thing about luck is you have to be
ready for it when it arrives.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.090
And that is where craft comes in.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:41.210
Yes…I remember…I got just plain lucky
several times along the way.

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:50.210
But then I had a closet full of work that
I could use and prove and verify that I was

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:54.620
really good at this and that I was the one
worth taking a shot with.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.150
It’s not just luck.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.970
You can’t blame it on luck if you’re not
making headway.

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:08.500
It takes a lot of time and you have to be
improving your craft FOREVER!

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:14.700
The thing about screenwriting and the best
ones will say this (I’ve heard it from the

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.510
best ones), “You never master this craft…never.”

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:22.990
It’s too intricate, it’s too intricate.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:30.020
But if you master your tools, you can continue
to have pleasure in it, live in it.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.390
The lives I’ve lived…I’ve hundreds of
lives.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:39.320
It’s a very rewarding thing to do even when
it’s rough.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:44.020
Even when it’s not selling.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:49.400
But it’s well worth doing.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:58.140
And when luck comes and if you’re around
long enough, if you’re around it long enough,

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.530
opportunity will come.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:06.840
But you must be ready to make the maximum
for that opportunity.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.780
No deer in the headlight time, you know?

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:14.260
When the headlights hit, you are ready to
tap dance and you’ve got your scripts.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:19.010
You have studied pitching, you know how to
pitch.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:22.530
We haven’t really gone into that but that
is really the key to this business and there

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.640
is really no way around it.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.920
I would recommend my good friend Michael Hauge’s
book.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.310
What is it, Selling Your Story in 60 Seconds
is approximately his title.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:40.120
You’ve got to know this stuff and I don’t
care if you’re shy, you can still find a

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:45.390
way to sell your story.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:54.030
And there’s this thing, a story (and old
story) that goes around that when we’re

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:56.380
practicing working on pitching in courses
and stuff.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:03.220
And people sometimes having fun, other people
are upset by it and I say “Just be as nervous

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.020
as you want to be.”

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:08.520
Pitching is not about being nervous, it’s
really not.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:14.200
I’ve said once upon a time there was this
creative executive that worked for a very

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:22.040
powerful producer and he comes bursting into
the producer’s office one day and he said

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:28.599
“Alice I just heard the most dynamic story
I’ve heard in five years.

00:03:28.599 --> 00:03:29.670
It’s perfect.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:32.250
There’s a role for a star.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:37.569
It’s funny, it’s touching, it’s just
the best story I’ve heard for ages.

00:03:37.569 --> 00:03:42.510
But I had to pass because the writer was nervous.”

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:48.379
They don’t give a damn.

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:57.720
If you have trouble pitching and you’re
doing something that everything in you wants

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:04.070
to resist, tell them your story, whoever you
are.

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:07.269
There is room in this business for everybody.

00:04:07.269 --> 00:04:11.129
Tell them your story, be as nervous as you
want to be.

00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:13.720
They are in the story business.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:20.669
If your story is ready, if you’ve chipped
away and shaped it into a patchable form (really

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:27.490
powerful story) and if your timing in life
is right, don’t worry, it will take care

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:28.120
of itself.

